The output side is typed as `$InferObjectOutput`, but `handlePropertiesResult` validates each child result and then discards it — nothing is ever written back to the input object. That's deliberate and documented ("validated but never written back"), but the type doesn't say so, and every shape entry whose output differs from its input now lies:

```ts
z.properties({ a: z.string().default("x") }).parse({});
// type: { a: string }   actual: {}          — no `a` key at all

z.properties({ a: z.string().catch("fb") }).parse({ a: 123 } as any);
// type: { a: string }   actual: { a: 123 }  — a number

z.properties({ a: z.string().transform((s) => s.length) }).parse({ a: "hi" });
// type: { a: number }   actual: { a: "hi" }  — still a string
```

This undercuts the PR's headline motivation. The existing test (`z.properties discards transformed output`) uses `z.string().transform(s => s.toUpperCase())`, a same-type transform, so it passes without exercising any of this.

Inferring the **input** side in both positions would be truthful for every case above, and it leaves the documented `URL & { protocol: "https:" }` example unchanged since plain schemas have identical input and output.

