This case never reads `def.when`. `$ZodPropertiesDef` extends `$ZodCheckDef` and `$ZodPropertiesParams` doesn't omit `when`, so `z.properties(shape, { when })` is a publicly typed call — and because `$ZodProperties` inserts itself into its own `checks` array, `runChecks` gates the shape assertion on `when` at runtime. `generateChecks` handles this correctly for the check role by throwing `ZodCompileUnsupportedError` (line 354); the schema role just runs the checks unconditionally.

At the top level that's masked by `compile()`'s whole-function fallback. Inside a union it isn't — each branch compiles to its own IIFE, so the wrong rejection is absorbed as "branch failed" and a later branch matches instead:

```ts
const p = z.properties({ a: z.literal("x") }, { when: () => false });
const s = z.union([p, z.object({ b: z.string() })]);

s.safeParse({ a: "wrong", b: "hello" });
// interpreted: { a: "wrong", b: "hello" }  (first branch, identity preserved)
z.compile(s).safeParse({ a: "wrong", b: "hello" });
// compiled:    { b: "hello" }              (second branch, `a` dropped)
```

Both report success, with materially different data and no fallback. Either honor `when` here or throw `ZodCompileUnsupportedError` the way the check role does.

